      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before becoming Christiana, the borough was known as Nobleville, later renamed for Christiana Noble, a pioneer settler’s wife.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christiana%2C_Pennsylvania",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1834, Christiana erected the world’s oldest free‑standing stone railroad archway, a marvel of early engineering.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.christianahistoricalsociety.com/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Every September, the Christiana Historical Society opens its doors for a free Open House at Zerchers Hotel, inviting locals to explore the town’s past.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
